                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the demand and the network`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Twelve months of demand sit in front of you for a basket of <strong>40 SKUs</strong> across fast, medium and slow movers, with current safety stocks, reorder points, supplier lead times and per-SKU fill rate. Nobody owns how the buffer is split between the CDC and the four RSPs — each analyst sets it by gut. Your job this round is <em>diagnosis</em>: classify the SKUs by volume <em>and</em> variability, locate where the <strong>€5.8M of dead buffer</strong> is hiding, and find the SKUs actually driving the fill-rate miss. The paradox to explain: service is too low <em>and</em> inventory is too high — at the same time.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Service targets and the safety-stock formula`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Turn the diagnosis into a policy. Set a target <strong>cycle service level</strong> per segment, then size the buffer with <strong>SS = z × σ</strong> over the demand-during-lead-time window, and translate it into a <strong>reorder point (ROP = demand over lead time + safety stock)</strong>. Test how the z-factor moves both fill rate and holding cost — the discovery is that service is <em>non-linear</em>: pushing slow lumpy lines from 95% to 98% costs far more buffer per point than the fast movers. The contract is written on the <em>fast</em> movers’ 24-hour promise.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Place the stock across two echelons`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The core call. Decide how much safety stock sits at the <strong>CDC</strong> versus the four <strong>RSPs</strong>, exploiting <em>risk pooling</em>: variability consolidated centrally needs less total buffer than the same demand buffered four times at the regions (the square-root law). Centralizing protects cash and turns but adds a transfer day; forward-deployment protects the 24-hour promise but multiplies buffer. And the hard constraint stands — fund the redeployment from the <strong>€5.8M of dead stock</strong>, not by buying more. Optimize the <em>network</em>, not four separate corners.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Stress-test and defend the plan`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Two shocks land. A key <strong>supplier’s lead time doubles</strong> from 3 to 6 weeks on a fast-moving bearing family, and <strong>Düsseldorf demand spikes +40%</strong>. Show the policy degrades gracefully — recompute the exposed reorder points, decide what to hold and what to relax, and lean on the central pool. Then give the board your 3-minute defence: fill rate restored above 95–97%, net inventory <em>not increased</em>, turns recovered toward 4.1, and the 3PL outsourcing made unnecessary.`,

                    r1_segment_label:       `How do you segment the 40-SKU basket to find where the buffer is misplaced?`,
                    r1_dead_label:          `Where is the €5.8M of dead buffer hiding, and why?`,
                    r1_offender_label:      `Which SKUs are driving the fill-rate miss?`,
                    r1_focus_label:         `How much of this cycle’s effort do you commit to the diagnosis before acting? (%)`,
                    r1_focus_readout:       `Effort spent reading demand & the network:`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    seg_volume:             `By annual volume alone (classic ABC) — rank SKUs by euros moved and stop there`,
                    seg_cv:                 `By volume AND variability (CoV) — an ABC × coefficient-of-variation grid, because lumpy demand is what drives buffer`,
                    seg_margin:             `By gross margin — protect the most profitable lines first, ignore variability`,
                    dead_cdc:               `At the CDC — central stock always grows largest, so that is where the fat is`,
                    dead_rsp:               `In slow, lumpy SKUs buffered independently at all four RSPs — variability paid for four times over`,
                    dead_none:              `It isn’t really dead — every euro of buffer is protecting some customer, leave it alone`,
                    off_slow:               `The slow movers — they stock out most often because nobody watches them`,
                    off_fast:               `The fast, service-critical movers at the RSPs that are under-buffered while cash sits in slow stock`,
                    off_all:                `All SKUs equally — the whole network is simply short of stock`,

                    r2_policy_label:        `Set the cycle-service-level policy across segments (this sets the z-factor and the buffer).`,
                    r2_rop_label:           `How do you build the reorder points (ROP = demand-during-lead-time + safety stock)?`,
                    r2_marginal_label:      `You test the marginal cost of each service point. How do you read it?`,
                    r2_csl_label:           `Target cycle service level for the fast, service-critical segment (%).`,
                    r2_csl_hint:            `z≈1.65 at 95%, z≈2.05 at 98%. The 24-hour contract is written on the fast movers — under-buffer them and the penalty fires; over-buffer the slow lumpy lines and turns collapse.`,
                    pol_uniform98:          `Uniform 98% on everything — push the z-factor up everywhere and the fill rate looks great`,
                    pol_uniform90:          `Uniform 90% on everything — keep buffers cheap across the board to protect cash`,
                    pol_diff:               `Differentiated — 98% on fast service-critical, ~95% on medium, ~90% on slow lumpy lines`,
                    rop_avg:                `On average demand over average lead time — the mean covers the typical case`,
                    rop_demandonly:         `Cover demand variability only — σ of demand, assume lead time is fixed at 3 weeks`,
                    rop_ddlt:               `On demand-during-lead-time variability — σ over the lead-time window, covering BOTH demand and lead-time uncertainty`,
                    marg_linear:            `Service cost is roughly linear — a point of service costs about the same on every SKU`,
                    marg_nonlinear:         `Non-linear — the last points on slow lumpy SKUs cost ~3× the buffer of the same points on fast SKUs`,

                    r3_split_label:         `The core call: where do you place the safety stock across the two echelons?`,
                    r3_funding_label:       `How do you fund the redeployment under the cash-neutral constraint?`,
                    r3_coord_label:         `Who optimizes the plan — and at what level?`,
                    r3_pool_label:          `Share of slow, lumpy SKUs you consolidate at the CDC to exploit the square-root law (%).`,
                    r3_pool_readout:        `Lumpy variability pooled centrally:`,
                    r3_pct:                 `%`,
                    split_forward:          `Push everything forward to the four RSPs — protect the 24-hour promise above all, buffer each region fully`,
                    split_central:          `Centralize everything at the CDC — minimum total stock, accept the transfer day on every order`,
                    split_pooled:           `Pool the slow lumpy lines at the CDC (risk pooling) and forward-deploy only the fast stable lines to the RSPs`,
                    fund_buy:               `Buy more stock to lift fill rate — add fresh inventory on top of what’s there`,
                    fund_redeploy:          `Redeploy the €5.8M of dead RSP buffer — liquidate the misplaced stock and reposition it; add no net working capital`,
                    fund_cut:               `Cut inventory across the board to free cash, then hope service holds`,
                    coord_silos:            `Each role optimizes their own echelon — the CDC team minimizes CDC stock, the regions minimize RSP stock`,
                    coord_network:          `One owner optimizes the network jointly — CDC and RSP buffers set together so the handoff isn’t under-protected`,

                    r4_supplier_label:      `Shock 1 — a key bearing supplier’s lead time doubles from 3 to 6 weeks. Your response.`,
                    r4_spike_label:         `Shock 2 — Düsseldorf demand spikes +40%. How do you absorb it?`,
                    r4_board_label:         `The 3-minute board defence — what do you lead with?`,
                    r4_rule_label:          `The one policy rule you institutionalize so the paradox never recurs.`,
                    sup_ignore:             `Hold the existing ROPs — the average still covers most weeks, ride it out`,
                    sup_recompute:          `Recompute the exposed ROPs for the longer, more variable lead time and stage a temporary forward buffer on that family`,
                    sup_panic:              `Emergency-buy that whole bearing family everywhere at once to be safe`,
                    spike_ship:             `Lateral-ship from the pooled CDC stock and the other RSPs — the central pool was built for exactly this`,
                    spike_local:            `Permanently rebuild a large local buffer at Düsseldorf for the spike`,
                    spike_relax:            `Relax the target everywhere and let Düsseldorf stock out until demand settles`,
                    board_service:          `Service only — “fill rate is back above 97%”, leave the working capital out of it`,
                    board_cash:             `Cash only — “we freed working capital”, leave the fill rate out of it`,
                    board_frontier:         `The frontier — service restored above 95–97%, net inventory NOT increased, turns recovered toward 4.1, 3PL made unnecessary`,
                    rule_moremax:           `“When in doubt, raise max stock” — a fuller shelf is always safer`,
                    rule_localowns:         `“Each region owns its own buffer” — keep the decision close to the customer`,
                    rule_jointecholon:      `“Position safety stock jointly across echelons by segment, sized to variability — not by gut, not location-by-location”`,

                    warn_focus_range:       `Diagnosis effort must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_csl_range:         `The cycle service level must be between 80 and 99 percent.`,
                    warn_pool_range:        `The pooled share must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_pick_all:          `Resolve both shocks, the board defence and the policy rule before committing.`,

                    coach_default:          `Policy logged.`,
                    coach_r1_cv:            `<strong>That’s the right lens.</strong> Volume tells you what moves; <em>variability</em> tells you what needs buffer. An ABC × coefficient-of-variation grid is what separates the fast-stable lines (cheap to protect) from the slow-lumpy ones (expensive) — and it’s the only view that explains the paradox.`,
                    coach_r1_volume:        `<strong>Volume alone hides the buffer problem.</strong> Two SKUs can move the same euros a year — one steady, one wildly lumpy — and need completely different safety stock. Rank by euros and stop, and you’ll buffer the calm lines and starve the volatile ones. Where is variability in your segmentation?`,
                    coach_r1_margin:        `<strong>Margin tells you what to <em>sell</em>, not what to <em>buffer</em>.</strong> The fill-rate miss isn’t about which lines are profitable; it’s about which lines are volatile and where their stock sits. Segment by volume and variability, or you’ll protect the wrong SKUs.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You read volume <em>and</em> variability, found the dead cash in the four independently-buffered RSPs, and named the under-buffered fast movers as the real fill-rate offenders. That’s the map the whole redesign rests on — and it explains low service and high stock at once.`,
                    coach_r1_deadcdc:       `<strong>The fat isn’t at the CDC.</strong> Central stock looks largest because it carries the catalogue — but it’s pooled, so it’s efficient. The dead €5.8M is in slow, lumpy SKUs buffered <em>independently</em> at all four RSPs: the same variability paid for four times over.`,
                    coach_r1_deadnone:      `<strong>“Every euro is protecting someone” is exactly how €5.8M goes dead.</strong> Finance flagged stock that never turns sitting at the RSPs. If it were protecting service, fill rate wouldn’t be 91.4%. Misplaced buffer protects nobody — it just traps cash.`,
                    coach_r1_offslow:       `<strong>The slow movers stock out, but they’re not what breaks the contract.</strong> The 97%-within-24h promise rides on the <em>fast, service-critical</em> lines at the RSPs — and those are the ones running thin while cash sits frozen in slow stock. Re-aim.`,
                    coach_r1_offall:        `<strong>“The whole network is just short” is the buy-more reflex.</strong> If everything were short you’d add €6M and fix it — but you’d violate the cash-neutral constraint and the board outsources you anyway. The miss is concentrated, not uniform. Find the actual offenders.`,
                    coach_r2_diff:          `<strong>Differentiated targets are the disciplined move.</strong> 98% where the 24-hour contract bites, ~90% on slow lumpy lines that cost 3× the buffer per point. You’re spending service where it’s cheap and refusing it where it’s ruinous — that’s how you lift fill rate without bloating stock.`,
                    coach_r2_uniform98:     `<strong>You just set every SKU to 98%.</strong> Fill rate looks great — and holding cost jumped and turns fell, because the slow lumpy lines cost ~3× the buffer per service point of the fast movers. The board asked you to fix service <em>without</em> adding working capital. Which segment actually needs 98%, and which only needs 90%?`,
                    coach_r2_uniform90:     `<strong>Uniform 90% keeps it cheap — and never meets the contract.</strong> The promise is 97% on the fast service-critical lines. Buffer them all at 90% and the penalty fires every quarter. A flat target ignores that service is non-linear by segment.`,
                    coach_r2_demandonly:    `<strong>Good differentiation, but your ROP only covers demand variability.</strong> The lead time also varies — and when the supplier slips, σ of demand alone leaves you exposed. The buffer has to cover demand-during-lead-time, both terms.`,
                    coach_r2_avgrop:        `<strong>Your ROP covers average demand over average lead time.</strong> Then the supplier slips from 3 weeks to 6 — and you’re out of stock before the order lands. Safety stock has to cover variability in <em>both</em> demand and lead time. Where is the lead-time term in your ROP?`,
                    coach_r2_lowcsl:        `<strong>A fast-segment target below 93% won’t hold the contract.</strong> The 97%-within-24h promise lives on exactly these lines. Set the z-factor too low here and you’re buying a penalty to save a little buffer.`,
                    coach_r3_pooled:        `<strong>That’s the multi-echelon move.</strong> Pool the slow lumpy SKUs at the CDC and the four streams partially offset — the square-root law means roughly half the buffer for the same protection. Forward-deploy only the fast stable lines that the 24-hour promise actually needs. Service up, cash down.`,
                    coach_r3_forward:       `<strong>You buffered every region independently.</strong> That’s four separate safety stocks for the same demand variability. Pool those lumpy slow-movers at the CDC and you’d hold roughly half the buffer for the same protection — the square-root law. Why pay to hold variability four times?`,
                    coach_r3_central:       `<strong>Centralizing everything minimizes stock — and misses the 24-hour promise.</strong> Push the fast, service-critical lines back to the CDC and every regional order eats a transfer day. Pool the lumpy lines centrally, yes; but keep the fast stable ones forward where the contract needs them.`,
                    coach_r3_buy:           `<strong>You added inventory to lift fill rate.</strong> Congratulations — and the board just outsourced you anyway, because more working capital is exactly what they told you not to spend. The €5.8M of dead RSP stock was your funding source. Did you redeploy it, or stack new stock on top of it?`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>Cutting across the board frees cash and breaks the promise.</strong> Indiscriminate cuts hit the fast service-critical lines along with the dead slow stock. The point isn’t less stock everywhere — it’s the <em>right</em> stock in the <em>right</em> echelon.`,
                    coach_r3_silos:         `<strong>Right placement, wrong ownership.</strong> If the CDC team minimizes CDC stock and the regions minimize RSP stock, the network is under-buffered exactly at the handoff. De Kok’s whole point is that the echelons are coupled — somebody has to optimize the network, not four separate corners.`,
                    coach_r3_underpool:     `<strong>You pooled almost nothing.</strong> The pooling benefit only shows up when the lumpy SKUs are actually consolidated centrally. Move more of that variability to the CDC and the same protection costs far less buffer.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>You held the old ROPs and the supplier doubled to 6 weeks.</strong> A reorder point built on average lead time is a snapshot, not a policy — it’s out of stock before the replenishment lands. This is exactly what a missing lead-time term in the buffer costs.`,
                    coach_r4_panic:         `<strong>Emergency-buying the whole family everywhere is the buy-more reflex under stress.</strong> You protected service by re-inflating working capital — the very thing the cash-neutral constraint forbids. Recompute the exposed ROPs and stage a <em>targeted</em> buffer, don’t carpet-bomb it.`,
                    coach_r4_ship:          `<strong>That’s the pool paying off.</strong> Because you consolidated the lumpy variability centrally, you can lateral-ship into Düsseldorf from the CDC and the other regions — absorbing a +40% spike without a permanent local buffer. Risk pooling is what makes the network resilient, not just cheap.`,
                    coach_r4_shipnopool:    `<strong>You tried to lateral-ship — but there’s no central pool to ship from.</strong> Because the buffer was forward-deployed and fragmented across the regions, the CDC can’t cover Düsseldorf’s spike. The pooling you skipped in Round 3 is what would have made this move work.`,
                    coach_r4_local:         `<strong>A permanent local buffer absorbs the spike — and re-traps cash.</strong> A +40% spike that fades doesn’t justify standing inventory in Düsseldorf forever. Lateral-ship from the pool for the transient, and you keep both the service and the working capital.`,
                    coach_r4_relax:         `<strong>Relaxing the target everywhere is surrender dressed as flexibility.</strong> Let Düsseldorf stock out and you hand the competitor the very fast movers the contract is written on. A policy that only works on average demand isn’t a policy — it’s a snapshot.`,
                    coach_r4_frontier:      `<strong>That’s the board answer.</strong> Service back above the contract, net inventory <em>not</em> increased, turns recovered toward 4.1 — you named the frontier, not one flattering number. That’s what makes the 3PL outsourcing unnecessary and keeps the nine planning roles.`,
                    coach_r4_service:       `<strong>Leading with service alone invites the obvious probe.</strong> “Fill rate is back — at what working-capital cost?” The board’s constraint was cash-neutral. Show them both numbers, or they’ll assume you bought the service and sign the 3PL anyway.`,
                    coach_r4_cash:          `<strong>Leading with cash alone invites the other probe.</strong> “You freed working capital — is the 97% promise met?” The contract is written on fill rate. A leaner balance sheet with a broken promise still loses the accounts. Show the frontier, both axes.`,

                    sum_default:            `Policy set.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Volume × variability; dead cash at the RSPs; fast movers as the real offenders.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Mis-read the segmentation, the dead buffer or the true offenders.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Differentiated CSL with a demand-during-lead-time ROP.`,
                    sum_r2_uniform:         `Uniform 98% — fill up, holding cost and turns blown.`,
                    sum_r2_avgrop:          `ROP on average demand — no lead-time protection.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Service policy or ROP only partly disciplined.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Pooled lumpy lines centrally, redeployed dead stock, optimized the network.`,
                    sum_r3_buy:             `Bought more stock — violated the cash-neutral constraint.`,
                    sum_r3_forward:         `Forward-deployed everything — variability paid for four times.`,
                    sum_r3_silos:           `Each echelon optimized alone — under-buffered at the handoff.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Placement partly right, funding or ownership off.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Recomputed ROPs, lateral-shipped from the pool, defended the frontier.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Held a static plan, re-trapped cash, or pitched only one axis.`,

                    insight_frontier:       `Frontier hit: you landed the efficient point — fill rate back above the 97% promise, net inventory not increased, and turns recovered toward 4.1. Low service and high stock turned out to be the same disease — stock at the wrong echelon — and you cured both at once. The 3PL outsourcing is off the table and the nine planning roles are safe.`,
                    insight_bought:         `You bought the service: fill rate is up, but you did it by adding working capital — exactly the move the board forbade. A fuller shelf reads as fixed until finance sees the turns. The redesign had to be cash-neutral, funded from the €5.8M of dead stock, not stacked on top of it.`,
                    insight_penalty:        `Penalty territory: fill rate stayed below 95%, so the service penalty fires and the board’s patience runs out. A third missed quarter ends the relationship with the top accounts — and that is the trigger that signs the 3PL contract.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed recovery: you defended the penalty threshold but didn’t fully reach the frontier — service and working capital didn’t both land where the board needed them. Enough doubt remains for the 3PL conversation to stay alive.`,
                    insight_cashneutral:    `The cash-neutral constraint was the real test, and net inventory rose. Buying or carpet-buffering your way to service is the one outcome the board explicitly ruled out — the €5.8M of dead buffer was always the funding source.`,
                    insight_pooling:        `Risk pooling did the heavy lifting: consolidating the lumpy variability at the CDC let you hold roughly half the buffer for the same protection (the square-root law) and freed the cash that funded the fast-mover service. That is the multi-echelon insight in one number.`,
                    insight_silo:           `Each echelon was optimized in isolation, so the network was under-buffered at the CDC–RSP handoff. De Kok’s point held: connected echelons must be planned jointly — four people each minimizing their own corner is not a network policy.`,
                    insight_rop:            `Your reorder points were built on average demand and average lead time, and the doubled supplier lead time blew straight through them. The buffer has to cover demand-during-lead-time — both demand and lead-time variability — or the first shock empties the shelf.`,
                    insight_diagnosis:      `The diagnosis was never quite right, which capped how high service could climb. Volume-and-variability segmentation, the dead RSP cash and the true fast-mover offenders were the map the whole redesign needed — get that wrong and every later move is aimed slightly off.`,
                    insight_turns:          `Turns recovered toward 4.1 without adding working capital — the clearest sign you fixed placement, not the balance sheet. Service and cash sat on the efficient frontier together, which is exactly the point the simulation argues.`,

                    hint_penalty:           `Fill rate is below 95% — the penalty fires and the 3PL gets closer. Aim service at the fast, service-critical lines the 24-hour contract is written on.`,
                    hint_cash:              `Net inventory has risen above where you started — that breaks the cash-neutral constraint. Fund service by redeploying the dead RSP buffer, not by buying more.`,
                    hint_silo:              `You’re optimizing echelons separately, so the CDC–RSP handoff is under-buffered. Set the CDC and RSP buffers jointly as one network.`,
                    hint_rop:               `Your reorder points cover only average conditions. Add the lead-time term so the buffer survives a supplier slip.`,
                    hint_pool:              `You haven’t exploited risk pooling yet. Consolidate the slow lumpy SKUs at the CDC to hold less total buffer for the same protection.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — service is above the contract and inventory hasn’t grown. Hold the frontier through the stress test and defend both axes to the board.`,

